\description{
\strong{Experimental.} For a patient at the start of (or partway through)
an episode of a gap-time recurrent-event model fit with
\code{\link{buildRecurrentEpisodes}}, predicts the distribution of the
\emph{number} of recurrences in \code{(prediction_time, prediction_time +
horizon]}, together with the probability of dying in it, by Monte Carlo:
every patient is simulated forward \code{n_sim} times, episode after
episode, until the window closes or the patient dies. \code{predictRisk()}
answers "is the \emph{next} event a recurrence or a terminal event, and
when"; this answers "how many recurrences".
}
\details{
Each simulated path goes as follows.
\enumerate{
\item The \emph{current} (open) episode's event time and type are drawn
with \code{\link{simulateTrajectory}}, from their posterior given the
patient's biomarker history in that episode -- the same distribution
\code{\link{predictRisk}} integrates, so the first step uses the
biomarkers exactly as \code{predictRisk()} does.
\item If the event is a terminal event inside the window, the path
ends (the patient dies). If it is a recurrence inside the window, the
count goes up by one; a stay length is then drawn (from
\code{stay_lengths}) and the next episode starts at discharge. If the
event (or the discharge) falls after the window closes, the path ends.
\item Each \emph{later} episode is drawn the same way, but with no
biomarker history -- the patient has not been observed in it yet -- so
from the model's prior given the patient's covariates, with the episode
counter advanced by \code{next_covariates}. Repeat until no path is
still running, or \code{max_stages} episodes have been drawn.
}
\strong{Limitation.} Only the first step uses the patient's biomarkers.
Later episodes depend on the patient only through their covariates (and
the episode counter), so any patient-level tendency to recur that is not
in those covariates -- a frailty -- is not carried forward. The patients
who reach later episodes are then mostly the frailer ones, the fitted
effect of the episode counter absorbs that selection, and rolled forward
it makes the expected counts too extreme: too high for the patients
predicted to recur most, too low for those predicted to recur least (in
simulations with a strong frailty the calibration slope of the expected
count was 0.7-0.8). Putting the patient's observed gap history among
the survival sub-model's covariates -- an indicator of the first episode
and the mean log length of the earlier gaps, say -- recovers part of it
(slope 0.83-0.9). Keep such covariates at their observed values
for the later episodes: the default \code{next_covariates} changes only
the episode counter. See the recurrent-events article.

The stay length is not part of the backward joint model, so it has to be
supplied. The default, \code{0}, means a recurrence takes no time (a
tumour recurrence, say); for hospitalizations pass observed stay lengths
(they are resampled with replacement).
}
\examples{
## Simulated recurrent events and a biomarker. Subject 9999 is still at
## risk in their 2nd episode, and is who we predict for.
set.seed(1)
n <- 60
data_events <- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(n), function(id) {
  k <- sample(1:3, 1)
  data.frame(
    id = id, time = cumsum(rexp(k, rate = 1)),
    status = c(rep(1, k - 1), sample(c(0, 2), 1, prob = c(0.6, 0.4))),
    age = round(50 + rnorm(1, sd = 8)), sex = id \%\% 2
  )
}))
data_events <- rbind(data_events, data.frame(
  id = 9999, time = c(0.8, 1.6), status = c(1, 0), age = 55, sex = 1
))
data_long <- do.call(rbind, lapply(c(seq_len(n), 9999), function(id) {
  tt <- seq(0, max(data_events$time[data_events$id == id]) + 0.2, by = 0.25)
  data.frame(id = id, year = tt, biomarker = rnorm(length(tt)))
}))
built <- buildRecurrentEpisodes(data_events, data_long, time_variable_long = "year")

survival_fit_recur <- survivalSub(
  built$episode_data,
  Surv(gap, event_any) ~ age + sex + episode,
  event_type ~ gap + age + sex + episode
)
long_fit_recur <- longitudinalSub(
  built$long_data[built$long_data$event_any == 1, ],
  biomarker ~ time_in_episode + age + sex + gap + gap * time_in_episode,
  ~ time_in_episode | episode_id
)

## The number of recurrences over the next 3 time units
open <- built$long_data[built$long_data$id == 9999 & built$long_data$event_any == 0, ]
counts <- predictRecurrentCount(
  open, long_fit_recur, survival_fit_recur,
  prediction_time = max(open$time_in_episode), horizon = 3,
  time_variable = "time_in_episode", n_sim = 50, bandcount2 = 30, seed = 1
)
counts
}
